  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German and translates to: 'very low probability of winning nothing.' The individual focuses only on the small (1%) chance of receiving 0 Euros in Lottery A. There is no mention of the reference value, outcome differences, comparisons to Lottery B, or any systematic evaluation of gains or losses relative to a reference point. The justification appears to minimize concern about the 1% risk of getting nothing, implying acceptance of Lottery A despite this small risk. No other outcomes, probabilities, or comparative evaluations are discussed.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating all outcome differences from a reference (2400), weighting them by probability, and comparing mean differences. The individual only mentions the 1% chance of winning nothing in Lottery A, which corresponds to a large negative deviation (-2400) from the reference, but does not evaluate this in the context of other deviations or compute any form of weighted average. The individual does not reference the gains (e.g., +100) or the high probability of matching the reference (66%). The justification centers narrowly on dismissing a single low-probability outcome, not on a balance of gains and losses around the reference. Therefore, while the reference point (2400) is implicitly acknowledged (via concern about losing it), the full structure of the decision reason — considering all differences and their probabilities — is not reflected. The verbal report lacks evidence of applying the mean difference rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

